ith type 2 diabetes were characterized by a moderate degree of gut microbial dysbiosis, a decrease in the abundance of some universal butyrate-producing bacteria and an increase in various opportunistic pathogens, as well as an enrichment of other microbial functions conferring sulphate reduction and oxidative stress resistance. An analysis of 23 additional individuals demonstrated that these gut microbial markers might be useful for classifying type 2 diabetes.","affiliation":"BGI-Shenzhen, Shenzhen 518083, China.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"agency":"European Commission FP7","acronym":"METAHIT","grantId":"FP7_201052","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Intestines","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Feces","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Humans"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Opportunistic Infections","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CL","qualifierName":"classification"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"CO","qualifierName":"complications"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","abbreviation":"MI","qualifierName":"microbiology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"PP","qualifierName":"physiopathology"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Sulfates","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","abbreviation":"ME","qualifie